    {
      "paperId": "/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-45573-5_3",
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-031-45573-5_3",
      "title": "Technology, Society and Economy",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Brian Harvey"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2023,
      "abstract": "Although space science was the most visible part of the space programme, the 1970s saw it transformed into a wide-spectrum programme to benefit Japanese industry, technology, the economy, society and later address the climate crisis. Here, the National Space Development Agency (NASDA) was established, importing American rocket technology to launch larger, heavier and ever more sophisticated satellites for communications, weather forecasting, technology testing, land and marine observations, the atmosphere and other applications. NASDA had its own launch site, Tanegashima and a growing infrastructure. Over time, Japan developed ever more indigenous and capable rockets which were regarded as among the best in the world. They were an instrument for the modernization of Japan’s economy and society, sometimes called ‘techno-nationalism’. The relationship with the United States caused initial friction, for the importation of American rocket technology came with subordinating conditions, restrictive controls, non-accessible ‘blackbox’ equipment, the prohibition of exports and licensing arguments. Over time, though, Japan was able to indigenize the imported technology and build its own launcher capability. Arguably, importation enabled Japan to progress faster than would have otherwise been the case, with Japan becoming the third country to reach geostationary orbit.",
