      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Mini- and microchannels have emerged as key components in advanced heating and cooling systems due to their high heat transfer performance, compact size, and low fluid volume requirements. This comprehensive review examines the fundamental principles, design considerations, and performance characteristics of mini- and microchannel devices. Fabrication techniques, including micro-machining, laser processing, and additive manufacturing, are discussed, emphasizing how they enable customized structures tailored to specific thermal management needs. Special attention is given to applications in electric vehicle (EV) cooling, battery thermal management, electronics cooling, heat pumps, and energy-efficient HVAC systems. The review critically evaluates heat transfer enhancement strategies such as surface modifications, micro-structured geometries, and phase change materials, highlighting their effects on thermal performance and system efficiency. Challenges, including pressure drop, flow instabilities, fouling, and practical implementation issues, are analyzed, with potential mitigation approaches. By synthesizing recent literature and identifying gaps, this review provides a fabrication-integrated perspective, linking design innovations with application-specific performance optimization. It serves as a valuable resource for researchers and engineers aiming to develop next-generation thermal management solutions that are efficient, scalable, and tailored to emerging technologies.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "Mini- and microchannels have emerged as key components in advanced heating and cooling systems due to their high heat transfer performance, compact...",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "openAccess_pdf": {},
